# Karpathy Guidelines

## 1 Think before coding
State assumptions explicitly. If multiple interpretations exist, present them — don't pick silently. If something is unclear, stop and ask.

## 2 Simplicity first
Minimum code that solves the problem. No features beyond what was asked. No speculative abstractions. No error handling for impossible scenarios. If you write 200 lines and 50 would do, rewrite it.

## 3 Surgical changes
Touch only what you must. Don't improve adjacent code or formatting. Match existing style. If you notice unrelated dead code, mention it — don't delete it. Remove only imports/variables your own changes made unused.

## 4 Goal-driven verify
Transform tasks into verifiable goals before starting:
- "Add X" → write test for X, then make it pass
- State a brief numbered plan for multi-step tasks
- Run `make test` before declaring done
